Reference
[1]  Java Collections Framework: Cite standard JDK data structures used for fast O(1) key-based lookup (java.util.HashMap) and dynamic element sequencing (java.util.ArrayList). 
[2]  Console I/O & Text Formatting: Reference standard input handling via standard stream scanners (java.util.Scanner) and formatted output strings (java.lang.String.format). 
[3]  Custom Exception Handling: Cite custom exception hierarchy implementation in Java by extending java.lang.Exception to enforce domain-specific error handling. 
[4]  Numeric Parsing & Validation: Reference standard string-to-primitive conversion methods (java.lang.Double.parseDouble) along with runtime error catching (java.lang.NumberFormatException).
[5]  Separation of Concerns (SoC) Pattern: Highlights how the code explicitly isolates core domain logic (the Account class) from input handling and UI presentation (the BankingInformationSystem CLI runner). 
[6]  Defensive Input Validation & Exception Guarantees: Demonstrates checking business preconditions (e.g., verifying amount <= 0 prior to mutations and catching NumberFormatException during parsing) to maintain data consistency and prevent invalid program states. 
[7]  In-Memory State Management: Refers to using state-driven data updates and ephemeral relational mapping via key-value dictionaries (Map<String, Account>) without requiring persistent database dependencies.     

Glossary
Terms	Acronym
CLI (Command-    Line Interface)	Text-based user interface used to interact with the application through terminal inputs and menu options.
JDK(Java Development Kit)	Cross-platform software development environment providing the standard library classes (java.util, java.lang) used throughout the code
SoC (Separation of Concerns)	System design principle reflected in separating the business domain logic (Account) from user interaction flow (BankingInformationSystem)
I/O (Input/Output)	Terminal operations handling keyboard inputs (Scanner) and console print outputs (System.out.println).
In-Memory Database	Ephemeral state storage structure represented by HashMap<String, Account> to look up accounts without external database management systems.
Custom Exception	Domain-specific error class (InsufficientBalanceException) extending java.lang.Exception to signal invalid monetary transactions.
Encapsulation	Object-oriented programming principle enforcing private access fields (accountNumber, balance) while controlling state changes via class methods (deposit, withdraw).
Type Safety / Generics	Parameterized collection types (List<String>, Map<String, Account>) ensuring compile-time check constraints on data structures.
Custom Exception	A user-defined error type (InsufficientBalanceException) created by extending Java's base Exception class to handle domain failures.
Source Account	The originating Account object from which funds are deducted during a transfer transaction.
Parsing	The operation of evaluating raw input strings and converting them into numerical primitives (Double.parseDouble). 

 3  Problem Statement
In the assigned problem statement 
In many educational and entry-level financial systems, managing account data, transaction logging, and fund operations efficiently without relying on complex, external database infrastructure presents a key technical challenge. 
To address this, the system must solve the following specific operational requirements:
	Dynamic Account Management: Provide a mechanism to create new bank accounts with unique account numbers and initial deposits, ensuring duplicate account identifiers are detected and rejected. 
	Transactional Integrity: Handle basic monetary operations—including deposits, withdrawals, and peer-to-peer account transfers—while enforcing business rules like preventing non-positive amounts or overdrafts. 
	Domain-Specific Error Handling: Robustly capture runtime errors (such as invalid non-numeric user inputs or transfers targeting the same source account) and throw custom runtime exceptions for insufficient funds without crashing the application. 
	Auditing and Statement Generation: Maintain a persistent, in-memory log of every transaction executed per account and offer an on-demand full statement view displaying active balances and historical logs. 
	Accessible User Interaction: Deliver a lightweight, menu-driven Command Line Interface (CLI) that allows users to seamlessly navigate between banking operations.
Functional Requirements
	Account Provisioning: The system must allow creating new accounts with custom account numbers, holder names, and initial deposits while preventing duplicate account IDs. 
	Balance Operations: Support positive-value deposits, balance-validated withdrawals, and atomic target-to-source transfers. 
	Auditing: Log every successful transaction into an order-preserved, account-specific statement log

Non-Functional Requirements & Design Constraints
	Data Consistency: Ensure account state updates occur in sequence to maintain zero balance discrepancies across transfers. 
	User Input Resilience: Gracefully catch invalid character strings (e.g., text entered into double fields) using try-catch blocks on NumberFormatException to avoid application termination. 
	Ephemeral Persistence: Utilize memory maps (HashMap) for fast O(1) object lookups during execution while keeping standard platform dependencies minimal

Future Scope & System Enhancements
	Database Integration: Replace the current HashMap implementation with a persistent database layer using Java Database Connectivity (JDBC) to retain data after program termination. 
	Concurrency & Synchronization: Add thread synchronization mechanisms to support simultaneous, multi-threaded user transactions safely.
	Graphical User Interface (GUI): Transition from a CLI menu-driven system to a JavaFX or Swing dashboard for better visual usability.
Existing and Proposed solution

Provide summary of existing solutions,provided by others, what are their limitations?
	Basic Console CLI Applications: Entry-level implementations leverage basic Java Standard Library collections (HashMap, ArrayList) alongside CLI scanners to perform core functions like account creation, deposits, withdrawals, and statement printing. 
	File-Based Systems: Mid-level implementations use standard file I/O operations (e.g., serializing Account objects or reading/writing .txt / .csv files) to store account data across program restarts.
	Database-Driven Applications: Enterprise-style or multi-tiered solutions integrate relational databases (such as MySQL or PostgreSQL) via JDBC or Spring Boot frameworks, offering persistent storage, user authentication, and RESTful APIs.
	Technical Limitations of Existing Solutions
Category	Technical Limitation	Impact
Data Persistence	Reliance on volatile in-memory storage (HashMap runtime memory). 	All account data, balance modifications, and transaction histories are lost upon program termination.
Numeric Precision	Usage of primitive floating-point types (double or float) for currency calculations. 	Introduces binary floating-point rounding errors over sequential financial transactions (unlike BigDecimal).
Concurrency & Thread Safety	Lack of synchronization or thread-safe datastructures.	Simultaneous operational requests on the same account trigger race conditions and inconsistent balance updates.
Security & Authentication	Absence of password hashing, role-based access control (RBAC), or session handling.	Anyone with console access can execute unauthorized transfers or view private balance statements.
Transaction Atomicity	Non-atomic transfer mechanisms between accounts. 	If an error occurs midway through a transfer (after withdrawing from the source account), funds can be lost without automatic rollback.

What is your proposed solution?
 .  Persistence Layer Integration: Replace the ephemeral HashMap memory store with JDBC and a SQL database (e.g., PostgreSQL or MySQL) to ensure account states and transaction logs persist permanently across sessions. 
.    Exact Financial Precision: Refactor all monetary data fields from primitive double variables to java.math.BigDecimal to eliminate binary floating-point rounding errors during financial calculations. 
.   Transactional Atomicity: Wrap balance transfer logic (transfer()) within managed SQL database transactions (COMMIT/ROLLBACK) or synchronized blocks, guaranteeing that funds are only deducted if the target credit operation completes successfully. 
.    Thread-Safe Architecture: Implement concurrent data structures (e.g., ConcurrentHashMap) or mutex locking on Account instances to handle concurrent multi-threaded requests safely without balance corruption. 
.   Role-Based Authentication: Introduce a secure login layer using cryptographic hashing (such as BCrypt) for account passwords, ensuring users can only access and view their own financial statements. 
.    Modern Interface: Transition from the console CLI scanner interface to a JavaFX desktop GUI or a Spring Boot Web REST API with a web dashboard for enhanced user experience. 

What value addition are you planning?
.   Financial Calculation Accuracy: Replacing double with java.math.BigDecimal eliminates binary floating-point precision loss, ensuring fractional cent calculations remain exact across large-volume transactions. 
.    Transactional Reliability & Data Integrity: Implementing explicit ACID database transactions guarantees atomic transfers—preventing partial operations where funds are deducted from the source account without reaching the target account. 
.    Permanent Data Retention: Upgrading from the temporary HashMap memory map to a relational SQL database ensures all accounts, balances, and historical statement logs persist permanently beyond application restarts. 
.    Multi-User Thread Safety: Introducing thread synchronization and concurrent data management enables multiple users or background services to perform simultaneous transactions safely without causing balance race conditions.
.    Security & Regulatory Compliance: Adding role-based access control (RBAC) and password encryption protects private customer records and restricts statement printing exclusively to authenticated account owners. 
.    Scalable Architecture: Refactoring the monolith CLI system into a decoupled architecture (such as a Spring Boot REST API) allows future integration with web dashboards, mobile apps, and third-party payment gateways. 


