5  Proposed Design/ Model
	System Design Flow Architecture
[ Phase 1: Input & Ingestion ] ──> [ Phase 2: Processing & Core Logic ]
                                              │
[ Phase 4: Outcome & Presentation ] <── [ Phase 3: Persistence & State Management ]
	Detailed Stage-by-Stage Breakdown
1. Entry & Input Ingestion Stage (Start)
	User Authentication & Sanitization: The user provides input via the interface (CLI/GUI/API). Inputs undergo standard sanitization to remove malformed characters before being routed to controller modules. 
	Input Parsing & Validation: Raw string inputs are evaluated and converted into strict domain types (e.g., parsing raw numeric strings into validated BigDecimal formats via try-catch exception logic to avoid program crashes). 
2. Core Processing & Business Logic Stage (Intermediate)
	Domain Precondition Verification: The system evaluates operational rules before executing mutations (e.g., verifying amount > 0 and checking sufficient balance thresholds prior to executing withdrawals). 
	Atomic Execution: Core logic operations—such as multi-account state updates during balance transfers—are wrapped inside isolated transactional units to guarantee atomicity (either both source and target operations succeed, or both roll back). 
	Thread & Concurrency Control: Mutex locks or concurrent locks are applied to targeted data entities to ensure thread safety when concurrent operations occur across shared state structures.
3. Persistence & State Management Stage (Intermediate)
	Audit Logging: Every validated operation generates an immutable event log record containing transaction parameters, timestamps, and updated balance metrics. 
	Database State Commit: The processed state changes and event logs are committed from the volatile processing layer to a non-volatile relational storage layer (SQL Database) to prevent state loss across sessions. 
4. Outcome & Presentation Stage (Final Outcome)
	Response Formatting: Processed outputs and confirmation states are formatted into readable output objects or formatted statement strings (System.out formatting or REST API JSON payloads). 
	Interface Rendering: The finalized state or feedback message is delivered back to the client interface (e.g., printed account statement display or visual dashboard updates). 

